  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 316 AM EDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\n===== Friday through the Weekend =====\n\nBy the end of the week and into the weekend the upper ridge is \nexpected to weaken and eventually start to retrograde westward as a \nclosed upper low over eastern Canada deepens a trough over the CONUS \nnortheast by Saturday and the weekend. A weak backdoor cold front is \nexpected to drop south-southwest out of Ohio and Indiana late Friday \ninto Saturday but trends in guidance continue to have this arriving \nmore later than earlier. This is increasing the confidence that many \nlocations across our CWA will still have to contend with the high \nheat and humidity for another day. With that said, the decision was \nmade to extend the current Heat Advisory until Friday evening. \nCurrent probability for Major HeatRisk is above 90 percent on Friday \nas we will still be dealing with afternoon temperatures in the mid \nto upper 90s and heat index values above 100 to 107 with dew points \nin the upper 60s to low 70s if we don't get afternoon mixing. The \nmain limiting factors to the heat on Friday would be a faster \nsolution with the approaching sfc boundary, development of any \nscattered to isolated showers and thunderstorms as well as placement \nof any potential MCS coming out of the Great Lakes late Thursday \ninto Friday and the path it takes along the edge of the upper ridge. \n\nBackdoor cold front is expected to work through the area on Saturday \nincreasing the chance for showers and thunderstorms Saturday \nafternoon and evening (30-40%). As was mentioned in the previous \ndiscussion, there will be ample instability along with steep low to \nmid-level lapse rates but overall shear remains weak (less than \n20kts) decreasing the potential for any organized convection. Albeit \n\"cooler\" than the last several days, highs will still be in the low \n90s to mid 90s with even a few upper 80s across the Bluegrass. with \na more east to west temperatures gradient across central KY. \nTemperatures to end the weekend will be in the mid/upper 80s with \nlow 90s across the west and southwest with just an lingering \nisolated chance of a shower or storm but looks mainly dry as the \nfront will be further south and the upper ridge off to the west.\n\nThe Labor Day holiday looks mainly dry and seasonably hot with \ntemperatures like Sunday into the upper 80s to low 90s across the \nCWA but deterministic models seem to agree that the ridging will \nbegin to expand and build back over the Ohio Valley for the middle \nof the week but may not have the same staying power as they models \nalso seem to generally agree on an upper level shortwave passing \njust north of the Great Lakes by the end of next week flattening the \nridge once again.",
